# JOS-251 — LINE ENDINGS FOR THE FILES THAT CANNOT TAKE A CRLF CHECKOUT.
#
# This machine runs `core.autocrlf=true`, so git checks a text file out with CRLF and normalises it
# back to LF on the way in. That works for hand-edited source. It does NOT work for a file a script
# rewrites: `scripts/scrape-spells.ts` writes exactly the bytes the MediaWiki API returned (LF) and
# `JSON.stringify` output (LF), so after every run the working tree holds LF where git expects
# CRLF — and `git status` reports 1,963 modified files forever while `git diff` correctly reports
# no change at all. That is the "stray noise in the tree" the integration rules forbid, and it is
# worst exactly where it matters most: the whole point of the revision-keyed cache is that a
# re-scrape produces a VISIBLY empty diff.
#
# `eol=lf` tells git to check these out the way the scraper writes them. The stored blobs do not
# change (they were already LF); only the checkout does.
scripts/sources/cache/** text eol=lf
src/main/data/spells.json text eol=lf

# .nvmrc joins them for a DIFFERENT reason. Nothing rewrites it; the problem is
# that nvm reads it as a bare version string, so a trailing carriage return
# becomes part of the version it looks for and matches nothing installed. The
# stored blob is already LF — this pin is about the CHECKOUT, which on a box
# running autocrlf=true would otherwise hand nvm a CR it cannot use.
.nvmrc text eol=lf
